\section{Details on RTE}
\subsection{Partial Contrubution Label Value Sensitivity}
We also conduct hyper‑parameter sensitivity analysis for partial contribution label values in the calculation of $U_t$ within the RTE system, with values ranging from 0.1 to 0.9. Subsequently, we estimate the consistency between the RTE values obtained from different partial values and human evaluations of trajectory quality, using Spearman and Kendall correlation coefficients to quantify the degree of consistency. The results are shown in Figure~\ref{fig:human_alignment_partial_contribution_sensitivity.pdf}.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.8\linewidth]{figs/human_alignment_partial_contribution_sensitivity.pdf}
    \caption{Hyper-parameters sensitive analysis for partial contrubution label value.}
    \label{fig:human_alignment_partial_contribution_sensitivity.pdf}
\end{figure}

It can be seen from the figure that the value of partial value has a weak impact on the final consistency, with a flat curve. Consistency only decreases when partial value is 0, the same as no contribution, and there is a slight decrease when the value approaches 1 of full contribution. This reflects the importance of the level of partial contribution.
\subsection{Correlation between RTE and Generation Quality}
As shown in Table~\ref{tab:rte-gptacc-corr-auc}, both Pearson and Spearman correlation coefficients indicate that higher RTE values are generally associated with a greater probability of correct responses. While the relationship is neither strictly linear nor determinative, a monotonic trend is evident across the dataset. The AUROC further in Table~\ref{tab:rte-gptacc-corr-auc} and Figure~\ref{fig:rte_lj_auroc_curve} confirms that RTE provides a moderate discriminative signal for distinguishing correct from incorrect answers. 
\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\begin{minipage}[c]{0.48\textwidth}
  \centering
  \captionof{table}{Correlation and discriminative statistics between RTE and final correctness (GPT-Acc) on the four Original RefAmb sources.}
    \begin{tabular}{lcccc}
    \toprule
     & Pearson $r$ & Spearman $\rho$ & AUROC \\
    \midrule
    Value & 0.3752 & 0.3287 & 0.7054\\
    \bottomrule
    \end{tabular}
    \label{tab:rte-gptacc-corr-auc}
\end{minipage}
\hfill
\begin{minipage}[c]{0.48\textwidth}
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{iclr2026/figs/rte_lj_auroc_curve.pdf}
  \captionof{figure}{The discriminative capability of the trajectory-level RTE metric in predicting final answer correctness.}
  \label{fig:rte_lj_auroc_curve}
\end{minipage}
\end{table}
Overall, these results suggest that RTE can moderately reflect answer correctness, though it is not strongly predictive. This limitation arises from the inherent complexity of real-world questions and the influence of hallucinated knowledge, which can lead to highly active pipeline trajectories that nevertheless produce incorrect outputs.
\section{Intervention Experiments Details}
\begin{figure}[htbp]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.9\linewidth]{iclr2026/figs/disturb_failure_to_ok_composition.pdf}
    \caption{Distributional analysis of the Disturb-recovered items (157 in total), showing (top-left) the proportion of items with LLM as Judge accuracy (LJ score) labeled as incorrect (0.0) versus correct (1.0), (top-right) the fraction of items identifiable from text alone, (bottom-left) the dependency on external evidence, and (bottom-right) the task-type composition across 5 tasks. The figure highlights that most recovered items remain incorrect, are predominantly multimodally dependent, and are concentrated in tasks requiring strong modality coupling and external knowledge.}
    \label{fig:disturb_failure_to_ok_composition}
\end{figure}
\subsection{Disturb-Recovered Item Properties}
We extracted whole items (157) from the Disturb Intervention in which the trajectory state transitioned from \texttt{generation error} or \texttt{max turn reached} to \texttt{OK}, and analyzed these items with respect to task type, final answer correctness, modality coupling of the questions $C_1$, and dependence on external knowledge $C_2$. Figure~\ref{fig:disturb_failure_to_ok_composition} summarizes the composition of the recovered items. The analysis reveals that, despite the trajectory status being restored to \texttt{OK}, the generated answers remain largely erroneous. Furthermore, items whose state could be recovered predominantly exhibit strong modality coupling and high reliance on external knowledge. This observation corroborates that the Disturb intervention enhances trajectory activity under conditions requiring real-world knowledge retrieval, yet does not effectively translate into commensurate gains in final generation quality. Among the recovered items, multi-hop tasks, comparison tasks, and entity attribute queries are most prevalent, corresponding to the categories with the strongest modality coupling and external knowledge dependency, as illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:stage1-label-2d-by-task-type}.
\subsection{Oracle Intervention Details}
Figure~\ref{fig:oracle-transition} elucidates the dynamic effects of Oracle intervention on trajectory states, providing a fine-grained view of how first-round query rewriting influences the evolution of agentic retrieval trajectories. 
\begin{wrapfigure}{r}{0.5\textwidth}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.5\textwidth]{iclr2026/figs/first_round_oracle_rewrite_transition_focus.pdf}
    \caption{First-round oracle rewrite state transitions.}
    \label{fig:oracle-transition}
\end{wrapfigure}
Predominantly, trajectories that initially fail—comprising cases that either reach the maximum number of allowed iterations or terminate in generation errors due to context overflow—are successfully recovered to the OK state, indicating that Oracle can effectively redirect failing reasoning chains by supplying precise entity grounding. Trajectories that were originally OK largely persist, highlighting that the intervention preserves existing correct reasoning without broadly disrupting stable paths. Nevertheless, a smaller subset of originally OK trajectories experiences regression, which suggests that while the Oracle intervention can ameliorate suboptimal retrievals, it does not fully resolve all structural trajectory failures, and in some instances, it can perturb trajectories that were already correct, likely due to over-specification or interference with the model’s intrinsic reasoning heuristics. The stacked bar chart further quantifies these transitions, showing the proportion of samples undergoing each type of state change, including recovery from failure to OK, partial improvement, or regression, thereby illustrating the nuanced and task-dependent impact of Oracle rewriting on trajectory dynamics.
\subsection{Analysis of More Indicator Results Under Two Intervention Settings}
\begin{figure}[htbp]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.7\linewidth]{iclr2026/figs/trajectory_quality_appendix_heatmap.pdf}
    \caption{Average step-level iterations (\#Iter.), information increment ($\Delta F_\text{avg}$), and utility ($U_\text{avg}$) for Original, Disturb, and Oracle interventions across task types. The top panel shows original-correct, while the bottom panel shows original-incorrect subsets. The heatmaps indicate that Disturb and Oracle intervention modestly reduce $\Delta F_\text{avg}$ and $U_\text{avg}$ for initially correct samples but enhance them for initially incorrect ones, with task-specific variations reflecting the interaction between query perturbation and VLM reasoning.}
    \label{fig:trajectory_quality_appendix_heatmap}
\end{figure}
In addition to reporting the RTE metric, we also recorded the step-level information increment $\Delta F$ and utility $U$ to enable a more fine-grained analysis of trajectory activity, as illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:trajectory_quality_appendix_heatmap}. For samples that were already correctly answered by the original pipeline, neither intervention produced substantial changes in $\Delta F$ or $U$ across iterations; however, both interventions yielded slightly lower $\Delta F$ and utility values compared to the original trajectory. This suggests that for questions the model can already resolve autonomously, excessive retrieval and reasoning may be counterproductive. Conversely, for originally incorrect samples, both Disturb and Oracle interventions increased the information richness of retrieved evidence, with Oracle generally providing a more pronounced improvement than Disturb in aggregate. Task-specific analyses reveal that the relative advantages of the two interventions can vary, and in some task types the performance order may be reversed.
\subsection{Referential Ambiguity Is an Inherent Phenomenon of the Pippeline}
Table~\ref{tab:oracle-rewrite-entity-ambiguity} preliminarily illuminates the residual referential ambiguity, providing insight into why referential ambiguity is a spontaneous behavior within the model. Across paired cohorts of original and Oracle-intervened trajectories, items containing at least one entity-ambiguous retrieval step decrease from 100\% to 59.35\%, and ambiguous retrieval steps drop from 77.89\% to 37.00\%. 
The persistence of residual ambiguity indicates that first-round oracle intervention does not merely rectify a one-off input defect. 
Extended trajectories increase the likelihood of hitting the maximum iteration limit (leading to Max Turn Reached state) or exceeding the model’s context window (leading to Generation Error state), thereby impeding convergence to a final answer. This dynamic underscores referential ambiguity as an \textbf{inherently recurrent property} of the model’s reasoning process, with implications that extend beyond the initial query reformulation. This can be reasonably interpreted as the misalignment between the characteristics of the VLM and the retriever. The VLM lacks sufficient understanding of the retriever's architecture, preferences and feedback, and fails to fully consider the capability boundaries of the retriever when generating queries.
\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\small
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{6pt}
\caption{Entity ambiguity before and after Oracle rewrite.}
\begin{tabular}{lrrr}
\hline
\textbf{Metric} & \textbf{Before Oracle Rewrite} & \textbf{After Oracle Rewrite} & \textbf{Reduction} \\
\hline
Item Scale & 100.00\% & 59.35\% & 40.65 pct \\
Step Scale & 77.89\% & 37.00\% & 40.90 pct \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\label{tab:oracle-rewrite-entity-ambiguity}
\end{table}

\section{Algorithm of IIGCR}\label{app:algorithm}
The proposed framework decomposes the agentic retrieval-augmented generation pipeline into three interrelated algorithmic components, each serving a specific role in managing trajectory integrity and query quality. Algorithm~\ref{alg:eao} formalizes the core IIGCR loop, which monitors each text-retrieval step and applies two gating mechanisms to detect potential trajectory stagnation: (i) query-similarity stagnation, triggered when consecutive queries exhibit high semantic overlap, and (ii) low information-gain stagnation, Detected when step‑level information increments fall below a predefined threshold using a simplified information increment: Perform sentence‑level chunking on retrieval results, and deduplicate current‑round chunks and historical chunks through two stages of string matching and cosine similarity, where string matching is conducted first, and cosine similarity matching is only performed for unequal chunks. Depending on the gate activation, the framework invokes targeted interventions to restore trajectory vitality or proceed with standard retrieval and update operations.
\input{iclr2026/appendix/algorithm_main}

Algorithm~\ref{alg:rebuild-messages}, the RebuildMessages module, operationalizes these interventions in two distinct modes. In the \texttt{missing\_info} mode, the module employs a large language model to generate candidate queries or responses aimed at recovering missing information, followed by a validation or rectification step to ensure semantic consistency and information diversity. The updated message sequence is then incorporated into the trajectory, either replacing the latest retrieval node or appending a new assistant node. In the \texttt{reflection} mode, a stronger corrective procedure is enacted, which prunes recent low-gain retrieval turns and directs the model toward alternative subproblems or clues, effectively enabling a controlled rollback and reorientation of the reasoning trajectory.
\input{iclr2026/appendix/algorithm_rebuild_messages}

Algorithm~\ref{alg:prune-trajectory-states}, the ContextPruning module, provides a low-level mechanism for maintaining message hygiene by selectively filtering out redundant or uninformative elements. It iterates through the message history, identifies assistant retrieval nodes corresponding to repeated queries or zero-information steps, and removes them from the active trajectory, preserving only essential and novel contributions. This module ensures that the trajectory remains tractable and that subsequent retrievals are evaluated against a concise, high-quality context, thereby preventing cumulative noise from impeding reasoning progress.
\input{iclr2026/appendix/algorithm_context_pruning}

Collectively, these three components establish a coherent framework for monitoring, repairing, and pruning agentic retrieval trajectories, balancing exploration with structural integrity, and mitigating the propagation of low-value or redundant retrievals while maximizing the effective utilization of newly acquired evidence.
